00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.789 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  you've probably heard a lot about  covid19 this year  and more recently you might have heard  about viral testing  what are the different types of tests  available what happens to your samples  after a healthcare professional  retrieves them from your body  and how can scientists use these samples  to tell  whether or not you've been infected by  sars cov2  to answer these questions let's take a  look at two categories of viral tests  direct and indirect direct viral tests  detect  physical parts of the virus itself such  as viral rna  or viral antigens a positive result from  a direct test  usually indicates an active covid19  infection  the most widely used direct test to  diagnose covid19  is nucleic acid amplification using a  reverse transcriptase  polymerase chain reaction or rtpcr  in rtpcr sars cov2rna  is extracted from the samples and  amplified to detectable levels  to do this a sample is taken from the  oropharynx  or nasopharynx then in the lab  reverse transcriptase enzymes take the  viruses  single-stranded rna and use it as a  template  to create double-stranded dna  next this dna is copied over and over  using pcr  until millions or billions of the  replicates have been produced  and can be detected this process is  complex  and requires specialized testing  equipment but automation allows for rt  pcr testing to show results within  hours however factors such as  sampling backlog limited availability of  testing kits or lab reagents  or long distances between the testing  site and the laboratory  can mean you don't actually get results  for several days  antigen tests are another type of direct  test currently used to detect  active covid19 infections antigens are  molecules on the surface of the virus  that trigger the immune system to  produce antibodies  which are unique proteins that attack  only the antigens of a specific  virus in an antigen test for covid19  a sample is taken typically from the  nasopharynx  and tank antibodies to the sars cov2  virus are mixed with the sample  if the tagged antibodies attach to the  antigens in the sample  this indicates the virus is present in  the sample  rapid covid19 antigen tests are  relatively inexpensive  can be performed on site rather than  shipping samples to a lab  and can return results in less than an  hour  however they are not as accurate as  nucleic acid amplification tests  on the other hand indirect tests detect  the body's immune response to a viral  infection  serologic tests for covid19 also called  antibody tests or immunoassays are a  type of  indirect viral test that searches a  blood sample for the antibodies to sars  cov2  if these antibodies are detected this  indicates a viral infection occurred at  some point in the past  and may or may not still be active  this is because antibodies are typically  produced one to two weeks  after the onset of infection and remain  in your body  after the active infection has ended  there are a number of antibody detection  tests currently being used for covid19  such as enzyme-linked immunosorbent  assays  and chemoluminescent immunoassays  in these tests certain colored  substrates  or luminescent molecules indicate the  presence of the antibody in question  scientists are still determining what  level of protection from  future infections sars cov2 antibodies  may provide  if someone tests positive for covid19  viral antibodies  they may be able to donate their blood  to those currently battling the disease  or to laboratories developing treatments  does a positive direct test result  definitely mean that you've been  infected with the virus and vice versa  not always important concepts to  understand related to viral tests  are sensitivity and specificity  sensitivity is the ability of a test to  correctly identify people who  are infected and specificity is the  ability of a test to correctly identify  people  who aren't infected in other words  tests that are highly sensitive have a  high true positive rate  and a low false negative rate while  tests that are highly specific  have a low false positive rate and a  high true negative rate  nucleic acid amplification tests for  example  are highly specific because they detect  the precise  viral genetic sequence but the  sensitivity of nucleic acid  amplification tests for covid19  can vary depending on the amplification  protocol  used as well as when and from where the  sample is collected  thanks to viral tests scientists and  health care professionals are able to  diagnose patients  so they can receive proper treatment and  can monitor the evolution of public  health crises  such as the covid19 pandemic with a  combination of increasingly accurate and  accessible viral tests  continued adherence to public health  measures emergence of medications to  treat infections  and development of vaccinations to  prevent infections  the current pandemic will eventually  come to an end  [Music]  you

